    "title": "Another massive streaming service is raising prices by up to 17%",
    "text": "LifeThe cost of the YouTube Premium service will increase from $13.99 to $15.99, a jump of 14%. Other services are also increasing. (Photo by Edward Pevos | MLive)By Matt Durr | mattdurr@mlive.comThe cost of a YouTube Premium subscription increased by as much as 17% Friday. The price jump marks the first time in three years that the video-on-demand streaming service increased prices for premium plans. The standard YouTube service remains free, however users must watch ads when viewing content. A premium subscription allows for ad-free viewing and comes with access to YouTube Music, the ability to download videos to watch offline and background playback.YouTube also offers a Premium music subscription and Premium Lite, which offers mostly ad-free viewing, but does not allow the user access to YouTube Music. A YouTube Premium Family Plan is also available. The price increases for each pack are:YouTube Premium -- Increases from $13.99 to $15.99YouTube Premium Family Plan -- Increases from $22.99 to $26.99YouTube Music -- Increases from $10.99 to $11.99YouTube Premium Lite -- Increases from $7.99 to $8.99YouTube Premium Student -- Increases from $7.99 to $8.99YouTube is the latest major streaming service to hike prices within the last year as parent companies continue to increase revenue through price jumps. In October 2025, Disney announced price increases for its Hulu, ESPN and Disney+ apps. That same month, HBO Max unveiled price increases for its ad-free and ad-supported tiers. Paramount+ kicked off 2026 by raising prices in January, just weeks before it announced a deal to acquire Warner Bros. Last month, Netflix confirmed it too is raising prices, weeks after receiving a $2.8 billion payment from Paramount+ as part of the deal that involved Warner Bros. $110,000 lottery ticket sold in Michigan expires next month: Here’s where it was soldWinning lottery ticket worth $78 million expires soon: Where was it sold?Detroit liquor store sells $1 million instant lottery winnerGoogle settlement with Android users worth up to $100 per person: Here’s how to file your claimMatt has been part of MLive's trending news team since 2016. He covers a range of topics while focusing on retail, development, the IRS, Social Security, music and celebrity news, and national politics....",
